WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.350
Our favorite horror movies often have more
going on than just a bunch of monsters preying

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.220
on ill-fated teenagers.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.820
Directors occasionally offer nods to other
entries in the genre, sometimes in the form

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.590
of a fourth-wall-breaking Easter egg.

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:15.240
Keep a silver bullet and some holy water handy,
because these are some of the best hidden

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.180
details in horror movies.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.160
Shaun and George

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.820
Shaun of the Dead is basically a 99-minute
homage to the horror genre.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.100
Obviously, the movie's title is a play on
George Romero's second big zombie flick, Dawn

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:27.980
of the Dead.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.000
Speaking of which, Shaun chastises Ed for
referring to the shuffling undead as the "Z

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:37.920
word"...

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.220
"Any Zombies out there?"

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:40.980
"Don't say that"

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:41.990
"What"

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:42.990
"That"

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:43.990
"What"

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.530
"That, the Zed word, don't say it."

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:47.530
"Why not?"

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.170
"Because it is ridiculous"

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:53.790
...which calls back to the fact that the characters
in Romero's movies don't call their antagonists

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:54.790
zombies.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:58.380
And when Shaun tries to book a date for himself
and Liz, his phone book features an Italian

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.950
food place called Fulci's—a reference to
zombie-film director Lucio Fulci.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:05.140
The name dropping continues throughout the
film.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:09.980
We see Landis Supermarket, a nod to the director
of Michael Jackson's "Thriller" video, and

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:14.170
Foree Electric, which is named after actor
Ken Foree, who plays one of the main characters

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:15.190
in Dawn of the Dead.

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:18.340
And we can't forget Night of the Living Dead's
most iconic line…

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.380
"They're coming to get you Barbra."

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:24.630
Which was referenced in Ed when Shaun was
talking to his mom Barbra.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.600
"We're coming to get you Barbra."

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.480
In return, Romero cast Simon Pegg and director
Edgar Wright in Land of the Dead.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.310
King Kong vs. sumatran rat monkey

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:38.210
Peter Jackson will always be remembered for
his Tolkien trilogies, but the filmmaker got

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:40.350
his start making B-movie horror flicks.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:46.200
He paid homage to his past in his 2005 King
Kong remake, which slipped in an awesome reference

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.410
to his 1992 gorefest Braindead, also known
as Dead Alive.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:54.850
The animal storage hold on the ship features
a crate labelled "Sumatran Rat Monkey—Beware

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:55.920
the bite!"

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.520
This is supposedly the same strange creature
that starts the zombie outbreak in Braindead…

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:04.850
...and in the movie, it's said to come from
Skull Island, the same island that gave us

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:06.230
King Kong.

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:07.810
Teasing The Dark Tower

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:13.080
David Drayton, the main character in Stephen
King's The Mist is a commercial poster artist.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.030
In the opening scenes of the film, Drayton
works on a painting of Roland from Stephen

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:21.670
King's Dark Tower series, which was later
adapted for a feature film starring Idris

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:22.670
Elba.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:25.800
Other horror movie posters can be seen in
the background of Drayton's office, including

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:26.800
The Thing.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.130
And speaking of The Thing…

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:29.620
Bad dog

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:34.730
The 1982 horror flick The Thing focuses on
a group of Americans working at an Antarctic

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:39.900
research station, where they encounter a shape-shifting,
parasitic organism that perfectly replicates

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.730
any life-form it absorbs.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:45.690
But if you understand Norwegian, the film’s
plot is spoiled before it even begins.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.790
As the film opens, a couple of Norwegians
are trying to slay a dog... which is actually

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:51.170
the shapeshifting alien.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:55.190
They shout warnings about the alien in Norwegian…

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:09.360
...which roughly translates to, “That's
not a dog, it's some sort of thing!

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.070
It's imitating a dog, it isn't real!

00:03:16.063 --> 00:03:17.693
With great power...

00:03:17.693 --> 00:03:22.243
Years after Hitchcock set the standard for
director cameos, Sam Raimi put his own spin

00:03:22.243 --> 00:03:23.483
on the tradition.

00:03:23.483 --> 00:03:29.433
Raimi's old car, a 1973 Oldsmobile Delta 88,
appears in the Evil Dead trilogy, Drag Me

00:03:29.433 --> 00:03:32.303
to Hell, and Darkman, among many others.

00:03:32.303 --> 00:03:36.892
The car remains Ash Williams' ride of choice
in the Ash vs. Evil Dead television series.

00:03:36.892 --> 00:03:41.002
And who could forget Uncle Ben riding around
in the iconic car in Raimi's Spider-Man?

00:03:41.002 --> 00:03:46.953
With great power comes great responsibility,
but with a 1973 Oldsmobile comes great cameos…and

00:03:46.953 --> 00:03:49.333
not-so-great repair costs.

00:03:49.333 --> 00:03:50.812
Raimi versus Craven

00:03:50.812 --> 00:03:55.703
Sam Raimi loves dropping references to other
movies, so The Evil Dead features an interesting

00:03:55.703 --> 00:03:59.942
detail: A ripped poster for Wes Craven's The
Hills Have Eyes can be seen in the basement

00:03:59.942 --> 00:04:04.273
of the cabin, right behind the table where
Ash and Scotty find the Necronomicon.

00:04:04.273 --> 00:04:08.903
This refers back to a scene in The Hills Have
Eyes depicting a torn poster of Jaws, and

00:04:08.903 --> 00:04:13.293
in response, Craven put The Evil Dead on TV
during A Nightmare on Elm Street.

00:04:13.293 --> 00:04:18.212
Lastly, Freddy Krueger's bladed glove can
be seen in the tool shed in Evil Dead 2.

00:04:18.212 --> 00:04:21.942
Raimi may have gotten the last laugh, but
we'll let you decide who has the better one-liners.

00:04:21.942 --> 00:04:27.092
"This is it Jennifer, your big break in TV."

00:04:27.092 --> 00:04:31.682
"Keep your damn filth bones outta my mouth."

00:04:31.682 --> 00:04:32.752
Talking heads

00:04:32.752 --> 00:04:37.673
In Stuart Gordon's Re-Animator, Dan Cain's
bedroom features a poster promoting the Talking

00:04:37.673 --> 00:04:39.363
Heads film Stop Making Sense.

00:04:39.363 --> 00:04:41.213
You know, the guys who did this:

00:04:41.213 --> 00:04:50.382
"Psycho, Qu'est-ce que c'est, fa fa
fa fa fa fa fa, better."

00:04:50.382 --> 00:04:55.083
The rock reference alludes to the antagonist
of the film, Dr. Carl Hill, who gets decapitated

00:04:55.083 --> 00:04:59.562
by Herbert West, after which Hill's decapitated
head and headless body are brought back to

00:04:59.562 --> 00:05:01.842
life by West's reanimation serum.

00:05:01.842 --> 00:05:05.692
Somehow, Hill's severed head is still able
to control his body, so — that's right!

00:05:05.692 --> 00:05:11.212
— we have a literal talking head in the
movie.

00:05:11.212 --> 00:05:12.212
Thanks for watching!

00:05:12.212 --> 00:05:15.802
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:15.802 --> 00:05:19.162
And leave us a comment to let us know which
horror movie Easter eggs are your favorites...

